The includes() :
    method in JavaScript is a built-in function used to determine whether an array or a string contains a specified value or substring, respectively. It is a case-sensitive method that returns a boolean value (true or false). 
    How to Use includes()
    The method takes one required parameter (the value to search for) and one optional parameter (the position to start the search from). 
    For Arrays
    The Array.prototype.includes() method checks if an array contains a specific element. 
    Syntax: arr.includes(searchElement, fromIndex)
    searchElement: The value to search for.
    fromIndex (Optional): The index at which to start searching (defaults to 0). Negative indices count from the end of the array. 
    
    const pets = ["cat", "dog", "bat"];
    console.log(pets.includes("cat")); // Output: true
    console.log(pets.includes("at")); // Output: false (checks for whole elements)
    console.log(pets.includes("dog", 1)); // Output: true
    console.log(pets.includes("cat", 1)); // Output: false (search starts from index 1)
    
    The String.prototype.includes() method checks if one string can be found within another. 
    Syntax: str.includes(searchString, position)
    searchString: The substring to search for.
    position (Optional): The position within the string at which to begin searching (defaults to 0). 
    const sentence = "The quick brown fox jumps over the lazy dog.";
    
    console.log(sentence.includes("fox")); // Output: true
    console.log(sentence.includes("Fox")); // Output: false (case sensitive)
    console.log(sentence.includes("lazy", 30)); // Output: true (search starts at index 30)

